1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,300
This is the key to a room
I rented in an Arctic town.

2
00:00:03,413 --> 00:00:04,838
I went back to return it.

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:06,163
The door was gone.

4
00:00:06,275 --> 00:00:07,400
So was the street.

5
00:00:07,512 --> 00:00:09,275
The whole town centre had moved.

6
00:00:09,388 --> 00:00:10,825
Hi, I'm Wob.

7
00:00:10,937 --> 00:00:13,787
I visit odd places and
ask why anyone stays.

8
00:00:15,012 --> 00:00:18,249
The town is Kiruna, in
the far north of Sweden,

9
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:19,687
above the Arctic Circle.

10
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,987
Under the hill next door is an iron mine,

11
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:25,275
and it is slowly swallowing the
ground the old centre stood on.

12
00:00:25,387 --> 00:00:27,887
So the town is packing up and moving east.

13
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,275
Houses, shops, a clock tower,
even a big red wooden church.

14
00:00:32,387 --> 00:00:33,699
I went there to move in.

15
00:00:33,812 --> 00:00:35,900
I even put the key on my own key ring,

16
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:37,437
next to my house keys.

17
00:00:37,549 --> 00:00:39,549
But one small fact kept bugging me.

18
00:00:39,662 --> 00:00:44,900
In seventeen thirty-six, a Sámi man named
Amund Amundson Mangi reported the iron ore

19
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:46,362
here to the Swedish state.

20
00:00:46,474 --> 00:00:49,062
He got a reward, and no taxes for life.

21
00:00:49,175 --> 00:00:51,587
The town didn't start
until nineteen hundred.

22
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:53,387
So what was everyone waiting for?

23
00:00:53,499 --> 00:00:55,274
My first guesses were terrible.

24
00:00:55,387 --> 00:00:56,212
Both of them.

25
00:00:57,936 --> 00:01:01,461
I arrived on a February evening and
rented that room in the old centre.

26
00:01:01,573 --> 00:01:05,374
A bed, a radiator, a glass
of water on the nightstand.

27
00:01:05,486 --> 00:01:08,786
At a quarter past one in the
morning, the glass started to hum.

28
00:01:08,898 --> 00:01:10,774
Then it shuffled a little to the left.

29
00:01:10,886 --> 00:01:12,061
I lay very still.

30
00:01:12,174 --> 00:01:13,573
So did the radiator.

31
00:01:13,686 --> 00:01:17,961
The mine blasts every night, between
one fifteen and one forty-five.

32
00:01:18,074 --> 00:01:22,248
The blasting area is cleared first, and
the tunnels are aired out for a few hours

33
00:01:22,298 --> 00:01:23,248
afterwards.

34
00:01:23,361 --> 00:01:26,161
Up on the surface, the
ground sometimes shivers.

35
00:01:26,274 --> 00:01:28,223
My glass shivered right on schedule.

36
00:01:28,336 --> 00:01:31,111
By the third night, I was
awake before it started,

37
00:01:31,173 --> 00:01:32,286
just to be polite.

38
00:01:32,398 --> 00:01:34,398
The glass and I had an understanding.

39
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:39,257
The next day I took the mine tour,
into the mountain next to town.

40
00:01:39,369 --> 00:01:42,519
According to LKAB, the
state-owned company that runs it,

41
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:45,470
this is the world's largest
underground iron ore mine.

42
00:01:45,582 --> 00:01:49,619
Its main haulage level sits about one
thousand three hundred and sixty-five metres

43
00:01:49,669 --> 00:01:50,469
down.

44
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:52,882
That's nearly four and
a half thousand feet.

45
00:01:52,994 --> 00:01:55,694
They take the ore out from
below, level by level,

46
00:01:55,744 --> 00:01:57,957
and let the rock above
cave in behind them.

47
00:01:58,070 --> 00:01:59,482
Think of an hourglass.

48
00:01:59,594 --> 00:02:03,232
Pull sand from the bottom, and
the top of the pile slowly sinks.

49
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:05,807
Kiruna's old centre is
the top of the pile.

50
00:02:05,919 --> 00:02:08,919
And the ore body keeps
running east, under the town.

51
00:02:09,032 --> 00:02:12,944
In May twenty twenty, the mine set off
the biggest mining-induced earthquake ever

52
00:02:12,994 --> 00:02:14,269
recorded in Sweden.

53
00:02:14,382 --> 00:02:16,094
Magnitude four point one.

54
00:02:16,207 --> 00:02:17,482
Nobody was hurt.

55
00:02:17,594 --> 00:02:21,019
That night I slid the key off my
key ring and put it in my pocket.

56
00:02:21,132 --> 00:02:22,645
Pockets are for maybes.

57
00:02:24,364 --> 00:02:26,064
So, back to all that waiting.

58
00:02:26,177 --> 00:02:27,927
My first guess was the sky.

59
00:02:28,039 --> 00:02:30,514
I stood outside one night
with a flask of tea,

60
00:02:30,564 --> 00:02:31,639
waiting for proof.

61
00:02:31,751 --> 00:02:34,514
The northern lights came
out, green and slow.

62
00:02:34,627 --> 00:02:35,789
My nose went numb.

63
00:02:35,901 --> 00:02:36,701
Lovely.

64
00:02:36,801 --> 00:02:37,664
And wrong.

65
00:02:37,776 --> 00:02:39,326
Tourism is big here now.

66
00:02:39,439 --> 00:02:42,127
In twenty twenty-four,
Kiruna had a record year,

67
00:02:42,177 --> 00:02:45,177
with more than six hundred
thousand commercial guest nights.

68
00:02:45,289 --> 00:02:48,152
But the town came first,
and the tourists followed.

69
00:02:48,264 --> 00:02:50,764
My second guess was the hotel made of ice,

70
00:02:50,814 --> 00:02:54,901
in the village of Jukkasjärvi,
about seventeen kilometres away.

71
00:02:55,014 --> 00:02:56,726
That's roughly eleven miles.

72
00:02:56,839 --> 00:02:59,476
I sat on an ice bed in
my coat to think it over.

73
00:02:59,589 --> 00:03:04,101
Icehotel began in nineteen eighty-nine
as an art gallery made of ice and snow.

74
00:03:04,214 --> 00:03:06,264
That's eighty-nine years after the town.

75
00:03:06,376 --> 00:03:07,264
Wrong again.

76
00:03:07,376 --> 00:03:08,576
My bottom agreed.

77
00:03:09,988 --> 00:03:13,475
The real answer rolled past me at
a level crossing on the way back.

78
00:03:13,588 --> 00:03:14,625
An ore train.

79
00:03:14,738 --> 00:03:18,688
Up to sixty-eight wagons, about
seven hundred and fifty metres long.

80
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,963
Roughly seven football pitches of iron.

81
00:03:21,076 --> 00:03:23,376
I started counting wagons and lost count.

82
00:03:23,488 --> 00:03:24,338
Twice.

83
00:03:24,450 --> 00:03:26,388
First, the ore had a flaw.

84
00:03:26,501 --> 00:03:30,600
It is rich in phosphorus, and in older
furnaces that made the iron brittle.

85
00:03:30,713 --> 00:03:33,076
In the eighteen-seventies, a new method,

86
00:03:33,126 --> 00:03:36,588
the Thomas process, turned
that flaw into a selling point.

87
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:38,163
That left the bigger problem.

88
00:03:38,275 --> 00:03:40,288
Iron is no use inside a mountain.

89
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:41,900
It has to reach the sea.

90
00:03:42,013 --> 00:03:44,838
Sweden's own coast is
to the east, at Luleå,

91
00:03:44,888 --> 00:03:46,326
on the Gulf of Bothnia.

92
00:03:46,438 --> 00:03:48,488
That sea freezes over every winter.

93
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:51,400
So they ran a railway the
other way, over the mountains,

94
00:03:51,450 --> 00:03:53,438
to Narvik on the Norwegian coast.

95
00:03:53,551 --> 00:03:57,725
Warm water from the Gulf Stream keeps
Narvik's harbour ice-free all year.

96
00:03:57,838 --> 00:04:01,413
The line reached Kiruna around nineteen
hundred and was joined up to Narvik in

97
00:04:01,463 --> 00:04:02,613
nineteen oh two.

98
00:04:02,725 --> 00:04:05,501
The king opened it officially
in nineteen oh three.

99
00:04:05,613 --> 00:04:09,726
Today, about twelve loaded trains a
day still run from Kiruna to Narvik.

100
00:04:09,838 --> 00:04:13,488
Luleå ships ore too, with
icebreakers helping in winter.

101
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,450
So the iron had been waiting
for a furnace that liked it,

102
00:04:16,538 --> 00:04:17,863
and a train to the sea.

103
00:04:19,076 --> 00:04:20,438
Then the town could happen.

104
00:04:20,551 --> 00:04:23,238
Kiruna counts nineteen
hundred as its birth year,

105
00:04:23,288 --> 00:04:25,226
when the king approved its town plan.

106
00:04:25,338 --> 00:04:27,863
The mining company's
first managing director,

107
00:04:27,926 --> 00:04:30,501
a geologist called Hjalmar Lundbohm,

108
00:04:30,551 --> 00:04:31,751
wanted a model town.

109
00:04:31,863 --> 00:04:35,013
He brought in a leading planner
to fit the streets to the climate.

110
00:04:35,126 --> 00:04:39,164
By nineteen ten, about seven thousand
five hundred people lived here.

111
00:04:39,276 --> 00:04:43,051
At the end of twenty twenty-five, the
municipality had about twenty-two thousand

112
00:04:43,101 --> 00:04:43,939
four hundred.

113
00:04:44,051 --> 00:04:47,188
They're spread over an area
about the size of New Jersey.

114
00:04:47,301 --> 00:04:49,788
And the mine still matters
far beyond the town.

115
00:04:49,901 --> 00:04:54,276
LKAB says it produces more than eighty
percent of the iron ore mined in the European

116
00:04:54,326 --> 00:04:55,126
Union.

117
00:04:55,038 --> 00:04:57,401
I stood by the tracks
with the key in my pocket.

118
00:04:57,514 --> 00:04:59,426
The train was still going past.

119
00:05:01,160 --> 00:05:04,535
I went back that August, because
a church was going for a walk.

120
00:05:04,647 --> 00:05:07,272
Kiruna Church is big, red and wooden,

121
00:05:07,323 --> 00:05:09,922
finished in nineteen
twelve, and much loved.

122
00:05:10,035 --> 00:05:11,597
The mine needed its ground.

123
00:05:11,710 --> 00:05:14,085
So on the nineteenth
and twentieth of August,

124
00:05:14,135 --> 00:05:17,873
twenty twenty-five, it rolled about
five kilometres to the new centre,

125
00:05:17,923 --> 00:05:18,985
in one piece.

126
00:05:19,098 --> 00:05:22,785
It rode on remote-controlled trailers
with more than two hundred wheels.

127
00:05:22,897 --> 00:05:26,135
LKAB put its weight at about
six hundred and seventy tonnes.

128
00:05:26,248 --> 00:05:29,910
To make room, the road was widened
from nine metres to twenty-four,

129
00:05:29,998 --> 00:05:32,447
which took a year, according to LKAB.

130
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:35,260
A year of roadworks,
for a trip of two days.

131
00:05:35,372 --> 00:05:36,710
My plan was simple.

132
00:05:36,823 --> 00:05:38,897
Walk beside the church, all the way.

133
00:05:39,010 --> 00:05:40,735
I overtook it almost at once.

134
00:05:40,848 --> 00:05:41,697
I stopped.

135
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:42,610
I waited.

136
00:05:42,710 --> 00:05:43,985
I overtook it again.

137
00:05:44,098 --> 00:05:45,948
It was slower than a slow walk.

138
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:47,748
I tried walking backwards.

139
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:49,198
Still too fast.

140
00:05:50,424 --> 00:05:52,849
The people along the
road had a better system.

141
00:05:52,962 --> 00:05:55,674
Swedish television showed
the whole move live,

142
00:05:55,724 --> 00:05:56,649
both days.

143
00:05:56,762 --> 00:05:58,749
Thousands of people lined the route.

144
00:05:58,861 --> 00:06:00,111
The king came too.

145
00:06:00,224 --> 00:06:03,111
So I stopped racing a building
and had a cinnamon bun.

146
00:06:03,224 --> 00:06:05,824
A dog sat down next to me
and watched the church,

147
00:06:05,874 --> 00:06:07,974
very seriously, the whole time.

148
00:06:08,086 --> 00:06:12,012
At its new spot, the church was turned
around a hundred and eighty degrees,

149
00:06:12,062 --> 00:06:13,762
so it still faces the town.

150
00:06:13,874 --> 00:06:15,237
It's still closed for now.

151
00:06:15,349 --> 00:06:18,012
The parish hopes to reopen
it in twenty twenty-seven.

152
00:06:18,124 --> 00:06:20,262
A town that carries its church across,

153
00:06:20,312 --> 00:06:23,974
instead of leaving it behind,
is a town that plans to stay.

154
00:06:24,087 --> 00:06:27,512
I took the key out of my pocket and
threaded it back onto my key ring.

155
00:06:29,244 --> 00:06:33,282
Eight days after the church parked,
LKAB presented a new forecast.

156
00:06:33,394 --> 00:06:37,369
About six thousand more people and around
two thousand seven hundred more homes

157
00:06:37,419 --> 00:06:40,306
would have to move, over
roughly the next ten years.

158
00:06:40,419 --> 00:06:45,282
Altogether, according to LKAB, the move now
affects about twelve thousand people and

159
00:06:45,332 --> 00:06:47,594
around five thousand four hundred homes.

160
00:06:47,706 --> 00:06:50,082
That's roughly two thirds
of the town itself.

161
00:06:50,194 --> 00:06:52,794
I held my key ring and
said nothing for a while.

162
00:06:52,906 --> 00:06:54,781
So what happens to a house in the way?

163
00:06:54,894 --> 00:06:57,882
I tried to imagine packing
a whole street into one van,

164
00:06:57,932 --> 00:06:59,344
with the mine paying for it.

165
00:06:59,456 --> 00:07:01,019
Homeowners get a choice.

166
00:07:01,132 --> 00:07:03,456
LKAB can build them an
equivalent new home.

167
00:07:03,569 --> 00:07:06,481
Or it pays the market value
plus twenty-five percent,

168
00:07:06,682 --> 00:07:08,456
set by an independent valuer.

169
00:07:08,569 --> 00:07:11,131
I asked myself what my key ring was worth.

170
00:07:11,244 --> 00:07:13,231
An independent valuer would have said,

171
00:07:13,394 --> 00:07:14,231
sentimental.

172
00:07:15,460 --> 00:07:19,185
I walked east, about three kilometres,
to where everyone was going.

173
00:07:19,298 --> 00:07:21,910
The new centre opened in
September twenty twenty-two,

174
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:23,223
with a three-day party.

175
00:07:23,335 --> 00:07:26,748
The new town hall is a round
building called Kristallen,

176
00:07:26,798 --> 00:07:27,610
the Crystal.

177
00:07:27,723 --> 00:07:29,935
It opened back in late twenty eighteen.

178
00:07:30,048 --> 00:07:32,710
Next to it stands the old
town hall's clock tower,

179
00:07:32,810 --> 00:07:34,497
moved across from the old centre.

180
00:07:34,610 --> 00:07:35,835
I stood underneath it.

181
00:07:35,947 --> 00:07:38,772
The clock had moved house
and not lost a minute.

182
00:07:38,885 --> 00:07:41,598
Then I went back to the old
centre to hand in my key,

183
00:07:41,648 --> 00:07:42,448
finally.

184
00:07:42,435 --> 00:07:45,410
Where my building had been, there
was a gap with gravel in it,

185
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:46,447
and a fence.

186
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:47,597
I kept the key.

187
00:07:47,710 --> 00:07:49,298
There was nobody to give it to.

188
00:07:51,016 --> 00:07:53,454
That December, I went back for the dark.

189
00:07:53,566 --> 00:07:57,816
In Kiruna, the sun stays below the horizon
from about the eleventh of December to

190
00:07:57,866 --> 00:07:59,328
the first of January.

191
00:07:59,441 --> 00:08:01,854
That's about three
weeks without a sunrise.

192
00:08:01,966 --> 00:08:04,466
At noon, the sky went pink at the edges,

193
00:08:04,516 --> 00:08:05,366
then gave up.

194
00:08:05,478 --> 00:08:08,666
January averages about
minus eleven Celsius.

195
00:08:08,779 --> 00:08:10,503
That's around twelve Fahrenheit.

196
00:08:10,616 --> 00:08:12,891
And the new centre has
a problem of its own.

197
00:08:13,003 --> 00:08:15,741
According to a University
of Gothenburg study,

198
00:08:15,791 --> 00:08:19,253
winter temperatures there can be
noticeably lower than at the old site.

199
00:08:19,366 --> 00:08:21,491
Cold air pools in the
dip where it was built,

200
00:08:21,579 --> 00:08:23,166
the way water fills a bowl.

201
00:08:23,278 --> 00:08:26,628
Narrow streets and tall blocks
keep the low sun off the pavement.

202
00:08:26,741 --> 00:08:29,503
On the new square, the
wind found me in seconds.

203
00:08:29,616 --> 00:08:32,266
The town's North Sámi name is Giron.

204
00:08:32,378 --> 00:08:35,954
It comes from the mountain,
and giron means rock ptarmigan,

205
00:08:36,028 --> 00:08:38,216
a small bird that turns white in winter.

206
00:08:38,329 --> 00:08:41,204
I would have liked to grow
feathers and sit in a snowbank.

207
00:08:41,316 --> 00:08:45,016
Instead I went back to my room,
slid the key off my key ring again,

208
00:08:45,153 --> 00:08:46,328
and put it in a drawer.

209
00:08:48,052 --> 00:08:50,664
I went back once more,
in June, for the light.

210
00:08:50,777 --> 00:08:53,389
From late May to mid-July,
about seven weeks,

211
00:08:53,439 --> 00:08:55,177
the sun doesn't set here at all.

212
00:08:55,290 --> 00:08:57,927
July averages about thirteen Celsius.

213
00:08:58,040 --> 00:09:00,602
At midnight I went for
a walk in sunglasses,

214
00:09:00,652 --> 00:09:01,839
which felt like cheating.

215
00:09:01,952 --> 00:09:04,289
I tried sleeping with a
towel over the window.

216
00:09:04,402 --> 00:09:05,627
Then more towels.

217
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:06,764
Then the wardrobe.

218
00:09:06,877 --> 00:09:08,714
The sun found the gap anyway.

219
00:09:08,827 --> 00:09:10,914
Summer here is short and serious.

220
00:09:11,027 --> 00:09:14,052
I took the key out of the drawer
and held it up to the midnight sun.

221
00:09:14,164 --> 00:09:15,014
It glinted.

222
00:09:15,127 --> 00:09:16,839
I didn't put it back on the ring.

223
00:09:16,952 --> 00:09:17,777
Not yet.

224
00:09:20,004 --> 00:09:22,842
Back at my desk, I put the
key down for this part.

225
00:09:22,954 --> 00:09:25,266
Moving a town is not only engineering.

226
00:09:25,379 --> 00:09:27,529
Most of the old houses are not moved.

227
00:09:27,641 --> 00:09:31,254
They are demolished, and for many people
the streets they grew up on are simply

228
00:09:31,304 --> 00:09:32,104
gone.

229
00:09:32,079 --> 00:09:36,554
In October twenty twenty-three, the tall
blocks that made the old skyline came down.

230
00:09:36,666 --> 00:09:38,116
Not everyone wants to go.

231
00:09:38,229 --> 00:09:42,054
In December twenty twenty-five, tenants
in one neighbourhood started a petition

232
00:09:42,104 --> 00:09:43,279
against being moved.

233
00:09:43,392 --> 00:09:45,542
And the mine presses on more than houses.

234
00:09:46,856 --> 00:09:50,146
North of town, behind
the mountain Luossavaara,

235
00:09:50,196 --> 00:09:51,086
runs a reindeer route.

236
00:09:51,136 --> 00:09:54,666
Gabna, a Sámi reindeer herding community,

237
00:09:54,876 --> 00:09:58,306
moves its herds this way between
summer and winter grazing land.

238
00:09:58,576 --> 00:10:01,746
Their old route along the lake was
cut off by the mine and the town.

239
00:10:02,076 --> 00:10:05,466
Now LKAB plans a rare earth
mine called Per Geijer,

240
00:10:05,516 --> 00:10:06,746
right on the route that is left.

241
00:10:06,796 --> 00:10:10,826
LKAB calls it one of the largest
rare earth deposits in Europe,

242
00:10:10,896 --> 00:10:13,086
and the EU lists it as
a strategic project.

243
00:10:13,136 --> 00:10:17,366
The company argues that electric cars
need the magnets these metals go into.

244
00:10:17,416 --> 00:10:22,186
Gabna's chair, Lars-Marcus Kuhmunen, has
said there is nowhere left for them to

245
00:10:22,236 --> 00:10:23,036
move.

246
00:10:22,516 --> 00:10:27,886
LKAB's chief executive, Jan Moström, has
said the problem is that both sides need

247
00:10:27,936 --> 00:10:28,946
the same land.

248
00:10:29,196 --> 00:10:31,246
According to the Swedish news agency TT,

249
00:10:31,296 --> 00:10:35,766
Gabna has asked the European Court
of Human Rights to look at the case.

250
00:10:36,096 --> 00:10:40,126
In December twenty twenty-five, it ended
its cooperation agreement with LKAB,

251
00:10:40,176 --> 00:10:42,406
saying consultations had changed nothing.

252
00:10:42,456 --> 00:10:45,106
LKAB said it would look
for a new way to talk.

253
00:10:45,376 --> 00:10:47,166
I don't have a neat line for this part.

254
00:10:50,984 --> 00:10:53,097
The key lives under my desk lamp now.

255
00:10:53,209 --> 00:10:54,097
Lamp on.

256
00:10:54,209 --> 00:10:55,022
That's June.

257
00:10:55,134 --> 00:10:57,972
Sun at midnight, a church
you can walk beside,

258
00:10:58,084 --> 00:10:59,847
a cinnamon bun at the roadside.

259
00:10:59,959 --> 00:11:00,872
Lamp off.

260
00:11:00,984 --> 00:11:02,059
That's December.

261
00:11:02,171 --> 00:11:05,659
Weeks without a sunrise, and a
new square that holds the cold.

262
00:11:05,771 --> 00:11:06,834
Lamp on again.

263
00:11:06,946 --> 00:11:08,272
I'm not made of stone.

264
00:11:08,384 --> 00:11:10,372
Kiruna didn't come here for the weather.

265
00:11:10,484 --> 00:11:14,296
It came for the iron, and when the
iron started pulling the ground away,

266
00:11:14,346 --> 00:11:16,759
the town picked up its
church and kept going.

267
00:11:16,872 --> 00:11:18,309
I admire that a lot.

268
00:11:18,422 --> 00:11:21,834
Not everyone got to choose, and the
reindeer still need a way through.

269
00:11:21,946 --> 00:11:24,096
As for me, I can't do the dark.

270
00:11:24,209 --> 00:11:25,171
Summer only.

271
00:11:25,284 --> 00:11:28,009
I'd come every June, help
carry something heavy,

272
00:11:28,059 --> 00:11:29,647
and leave before the sun does.

273
00:11:29,759 --> 00:11:33,097
If you like snow, quiet and a
project measured in decades,

274
00:11:33,147 --> 00:11:34,634
you'd outlast me easily.

275
00:11:34,746 --> 00:11:36,909
I hung the key on a nail by my window,

276
00:11:36,959 --> 00:11:39,471
pointing east, in case its door turns up.

277
00:11:39,584 --> 00:11:42,009
What would you refuse to
move, even for a mine?

278
00:11:43,724 --> 00:11:46,299
Up on the shelf, the key
gets a nail of its own.

279
00:11:46,412 --> 00:11:50,499
The postcard leans on the opal jar,
which still rattles mostly air.

280
00:11:50,611 --> 00:11:53,424
My socks are already
nervous about the next trip.

281
00:11:53,536 --> 00:11:55,411
I hear the rain there takes its time.
